Note that  $1 - 4 \sin^2 \frac{\pi}{3\cdot 2^{k}} = 2\cos\frac{\pi}{3\cdot 2^{k-1}}-1$  by the cosine double angle formula. Furthermore, we have that:
\begin{align*}
2\cos\frac{\pi}{3\cdot 2^{k-1}}-1& = \frac{\sin\frac{\pi}{2^{k-2}\cdot 3} - \sin\frac{\pi}{2^{k-1}\cdot 3}}{\sin\frac{\pi}{2^{k-1}\cdot 3}} 
&=\frac{2\sin\frac{\pi}{2^k\cdot 3}\cos\frac{\pi}{2^k}}{\sin\frac{\pi}{2^{k-1}\cdot 3}}
\end{align*}
By sine double angle formula and sum to product. Finally, 
\begin{align}
\prod_{k = 2}^{\infty} \left(\frac{2\sin\frac{\pi}{2^k\cdot 3}\cos\frac{\pi}{2^k}}{\sin\frac{\pi}{2^{k-1}\cdot 3}}\right) &= \lim_{k \to \infty} 2^k \cdot \frac{\cos\frac{\pi}{2^2}\cdots \frac{\pi}{2^k}\cdot \sin\frac{\pi}{2^k\cdot 3}}{\sin\frac{\pi}{2}} \notag 
&=\lim_{k \to \infty} 2^k \cdot \frac{\sin\frac{\pi}{2^k\cdot 3}}{\sin\frac{\pi}{2^k}} 
&=\boxed{\frac{2}{3}}
\end{align}
Where (1) follows from multiplying and dividing by  $\sin\frac{\pi}{2^k}$  and using the sine double angle formula and (2) follows from L'Hopital's rule

Multiply the product by \[\prod_{k = 2}^\infty \cos\left(\frac{\pi}{3\cdot2^k}\right)\]. Hence, we get \[\frac{\prod_{k = 2}^\infty \cos\left(\frac{\pi}{2^k}\right)}{\prod_{k = 2}^\infty \cos\left(\frac{\pi}{3\cdot2^k}\right)}\]
We will do each one seperately, with partial product. 
\begin{align*}
  \prod_{k = 2}^\infty \cos\left(\frac{\pi}{3\cdot2^k}\right) & = \lim_{n\to\infty}\prod_{k = 2}^n \cos\left(\frac{\pi}{3\cdot2^k}\right) 
  & = \lim_{n\to\infty}\frac{\sin\left(\frac\pi{3\cdot2^n}\right)}{\sin\left(\frac\pi{3\cdot2^n}\right)}\prod_{k = 2}^n \cos\left(\frac{\pi}{3\cdot2^k}\right) 
  & = \lim_{n\to\infty}\frac{\frac12}{\sin\left(\frac\pi{3\cdot2^n}\right)}\cdot\frac{1}{2^{n - 1}} 
  & = \frac3\pi \lim_{n\to\infty} \frac{\frac{\pi}{3\cdot2^n}}{\sin\left(\frac{\pi}{3\cdot2^n}\right)} = \frac3\pi
\end{align*}
By similar reasoning, we have that the numerator is  $\frac2\pi.$  Thus, the answer is  $\boxed{\frac23}$

Note that  $1 - 4\sin^2(x) = 2\cos(2x) - 1$ . We can therefore rewrite our product as follows:

\begin{align*}
    \prod_{k=2}^\infty (2\cos(2x) - 1)
\end{align*}

Because I'm doing this problem in Calculus class, consider a finite product  $P_n$  with upper index  $n$  as we take the limit when  $n \rightarrow \infty$ . Furthermore, set  $\omega = e^{i\pi/6 \cdot 2^{n-1}}$ , and consider rewriting trigonometric terms in terms of exponential terms. Then, we have:

\begin{align*}
    P_n &= \prod_{k = 2}^{n+1} \left\lvert \omega^{2^{n-k+1}} + \omega^{2^{-n+k-1}} - 1 \right\rvert 
    &= \prod_{k = 0}^{n-1} \left\lvert \omega^{k} -1 + \omega^{-k}\right\rvert 
    &= \prod_{k=0}^{n-1} \left\lvert \frac{\omega^{2k} - \omega^k + 1}{\omega^k} \right\rvert 
    &= \prod_{k=0}^{n-1} \left\lvert \frac{\omega^{3k} + 1}{\omega^k + 1} \right\rvert \cdot \left\lvert \frac{1}{\omega^k} \right\rvert
    &= \frac{\left\lvert (1+\omega^3)(1+\omega^{3 \cdot 2})\dots(1+\omega^{3 \cdot2^{N-1}}) \right\rvert}{\left\lvert (1+\omega)(1+\omega^2)\dots(1+\omega^{2^{N-1}}) \right\rvert} 
    &= \frac{\left\lvert 1 + \omega^3 + \omega^6 + \dots + \omega^{3 \cdot(2^N - 1)} \right\rvert}{\left\lvert 1 + \omega^1 + \omega^2 + \dots + \omega^{2^N - 1} \right\rvert}
    &= \left\lvert\frac{(\omega^{3 \cdot 2^{N}} - 1)/(\omega^{3} - 1)}{(\omega^{2^{N} - 1})/(\omega - 1)}\right\rvert 
    &= \frac{\left\lvert\omega^{2^{N+1}} + \omega^{2^N} + 1\right\rvert}{\left\lvert\omega^2 + \omega + 1\right\rvert} 
    &= \frac{2}{\left\lvert \omega^2 + \omega + 1 \right\rvert} 
\end{align*}

Taking the limit  $n \rightarrow \infty$ , we notice that  $\omega$  and  $\omega^2$  get infinitely close to  $3$ . Thus, we have

\begin{align*}
\lim_{n \rightarrow \infty}P_n &= \boxed{\frac{2}{3}}
\end{align*}

which is our final answer.

Notice, 
\[ 1-4\sin^2\theta = 1 - 4\left(\dfrac{e^{i\theta}-e^{-i\theta}}{2i}\right)^2 = e^{2i\theta} - 1 + e^{-2i\theta}. \]
Let  $X = e^{\tfrac{\pi}{3\cdot 2^{n-1}} i}$ . Then, we wish to compute the magnitude of the following:
\begin{align*}
\lim_{n\to\infty} \prod_{k = 2}^{n} \left( 1 - 4 \sin^2 \frac{\pi}{3\cdot 2^{k}} \right) 
&= \lim_{n\to\infty} \prod_{k=0}^{n-2} \left(X^{2^k} -1 + \dfrac{1}{X^{2^k}}\right) 
&= \lim_{n\to\infty} \prod_{k=0}^{n-2} \left(\dfrac{X^{2\cdot (2^{k})} - X^{(2^{k})} + 1}{X^{(2^{k})}}\right) 
&= \lim_{n\to\infty} \prod_{k=0}^{n-2} \left(\dfrac{X^{3\cdot (2^{k})} + 1}{X^{(2^{k})}(X^{(2^{k})}+1)}\right) 
&= \lim_{n\to\infty} \prod_{k=0}^{n-2} \left(\dfrac{X^{3\cdot (2^{k})} + 1}{X^{(2^{k})}+1}\right) 
&= \lim_{n\to\infty} \dfrac{1+X^3+X^6+\cdots+X^{3\cdot(2^{n-1}-1)}}{1+X+X^2+\cdots + X^{2^{n-1}-1}} 
&= \lim_{n\to\infty} \dfrac{X^{3\cdot 2^{n-1}}-1}{X^3-1} \cdot \dfrac{X-1}{X^{2^{n-1}}-1} 
&= \lim_{n\to\infty} \dfrac{X-1}{X^3-1} \cdot \dfrac{X^{3\cdot2^{n-1}}-1}{X^{2^{n-1}}-1} 
&= \lim_{n\to\infty} \dfrac{X^{2\cdot 2^{n-1}}+X^{2^{n-1}}+1}{X^2+X+1} 
&= \lim_{n\to\infty} \dfrac{X^{2\cdot 2^{n-1}}+X^{2^{n-1}}+1}{1+1+1} 
&= \lim_{n\to\infty} \dfrac{e^{4\pi i/3} + e^{2\pi i/3}+1}{1+1+1} 
&= \boxed{\dfrac{2}{3}.}
\end{align*}

