<details><summary>Solution</summary>The main claim is that  $AP$  is a symmedian; the rest is just computation. An alternative approach would be to show that  $BCXY$  is a parallelogram. It is already given that  $BC \parallel XY,$  so we just need to show that  $BY \parallel CX.$  Since  $\angle PBA = 180-(\angle ACB + \angle BCP)$  and  $AB$  is tangent to the circumcircle of  $\triangle BCX,$  we have that  $\angle YCX \cong \angle BCA.$  Similarly, since  $AC$  is tangent to the circumcircle of  $\triangle BCY,$  we have that  $\angle BYC \cong \angle ACB.$  Thus,  $\angle BYC \cong \angle YCX,$  which implies  $BY \parallel CX.$  Now observe that  $\triangle ABC \sim \triangle PXC$  by AA similarity. Therefore,  $CX=\tfrac{5}{4}PX$  and  $PC=\tfrac{3}{2}PX.$  By Ptolemy's Theorem,  $AP=\tfrac{12}{5}PX$  and it suffices to find  $PX.$  The Law of Cosines gives  $\cos(\angle BAC)=\tfrac{9}{16},$  so  $25={PX}^2+{\tfrac{9}{4}PX}^2-2PX \cdot \tfrac{3}{2}PX \cdot \tfrac{-9}{16},$  so  $PX=\tfrac{20}{\sqrt{79}}.$  Then  $AP=\tfrac{48}{\sqrt{79}}$  and  ${AP}^2=\tfrac{2304}{79}$  and the answer is  $\boxed{230479}$</details>

