Let  $m_s = \tfrac{m}{\gcd(m,n)}$  and  $n_s=\tfrac{n}{\gcd(m,n)}.$  Then, notice that\begin{align*}\text{lcm}(m,n)+\gcd(m,n)&=m+n+30mn + \gcd(m,n)^2 &= m\gcd(m,n) + n\gcd(m,n) + 30\gcd(m,n)
(m-\gcd(m,n))(n-\gcd(m,n))&=30\gcd(m,n)(m_s-1)(n_s-1)&=\tfrac{30}{\gcd(m,n)}.\end{align*}
Since  $m_s-1$  and  $n_s-1$  are both integers,  $\gcd(m,n)$  divides  $30,$  so now we have some easy casework.

