Not that hard for P6. I simply just guessed the explicit formula and prove by induction that it works.**Claim :** Let  $k\in\mathbb{Z}^+$  and  $a_n$  be a sequence defined by  $$ a_0=0, \quad a_1=1, \quad a_{n+1} = \frac{2k}{n}a_n + a_{n-1} \quad\text{for } n\geqslant 1. $$  Then the closed form is given by  $$ a_n = \sum_{i=0}^k 2^i\binom{k-1}{i} \binom{n}{i+1} $$  **Proof :** Clearly the closed form satisfies the initial conditions. Now we prove the recurrence. First, we rewrite the second term.
\begin{align*}
\frac{2k}{n}a_n &= \sum_{i=0}^k 2^{i+1}\frac{k}{n}\binom{k-1}{i}\binom{n}{i+1} 
&= \sum_{i=0}^k 2^{i+1}\frac{k}{n}\cdot\frac{(k-1)!}{i!(k-1-i)!}\cdot \frac{n!}{(i+1)!(n-i-1)!} 
&= \sum_{i=0}^k 2^{i+1} \frac{k!}{(i+1)! (k-1-i)!} \cdot \frac{(n-1)!}{i!(n-i-1)!} 
&= \sum_{i=0}^k 2^{i+1}\binom{k}{i+1}\binom{n-1}{i} 
&= \sum_{i=1}^k 2^i \binom{k}{k-i} \binom{n-1}{i-1} \end{align*}
This is coefficient of  $x^{k-1}$  of  $(x+2)^k(x+1)^{n-1}$ . But notice that  $a_n$  is coefficient of  $x^k$  in  $(x+2)^{k-1}(x+1)^n$ . Thus   $$ a_{n+1} - a_{n-1} = [x^k]\left((x+2)^{k-1}(x+1)^{n+1} - (x+2)^{k-1}(x+1)^{n-1}\right) = [x^k]\left(x(x+2)^k(x+1)^{n-1}\right) $$  thus this formula satisfies recurrence relation, so we are done.

The problem easily follows from the claim.

