      }
      fill(shift(1,3)*sq,red);
      fill(shift(2,2)*sq,red);
      fill(shift(3,1)*sq,red);
      fill(shift(2,3)*sq,blue);
      fill(shift(3,2)*sq,blue);
      for (int i=0;i<6;++i) {
      draw((i,-1)--(i,6));
      draw((-1,i)--(6,i));
      }
[/asy]

All red squares must be covered by a domino, and every domino through one red square must also pass through a blue square. However, there are fewer blue squares than red squares, so this is impossible.**Claim 26.2.** There cannot exist any cycle consisting of diagonally adjacent black cells.

*Proof.* Assume FTSOC that such a cycle exists.

  [asy]
        size(6cm);
        path sq = (0,0)--(0,1)--(1,1)--(1,0)--cycle;
        fill(shift((2,0))*sq);
        fill(shift((1,1))*sq);
        fill(shift((0,2))*sq);
        fill(shift((1,3))*sq);
        fill(shift((0,4))*sq);
        fill(shift((1,5))*sq);
        fill(shift((2,6))*sq);
        fill(shift((3,5))*sq);
        fill(shift((4,4))*sq);
        fill(shift((5,3))*sq);
        fill(shift((6,2))*sq);
        fill(shift((5,1))*sq);
        fill(shift((4,0))*sq);
        fill(shift((3,1))*sq);
        for (int i=0;i<8;++i) {
        draw((i,-1)--(i,8));
        draw((-1,i)--(8,i));
        }
        draw(shift(0.5,0.5)*((2,0)--(0,2)--(1,3)--(0,4)--(2,6)--(6,2)--(4,0)--(3,1)--cycle),red+linewidth(2));
[/asy]

Consider the polygon (highlighted in red) formed by the centers of these squares. If there are  $h$  vertices, every angle is either  $90^\circ$  or  $270^\circ$ , and they add to  $(180h-360)^\circ$ , so there are four more  $90^\circ$  angles than  $270^\circ$  angles, which means that there are two  $90^\circ$  angles in a row. Any two  $90^\circ$  angles in a row will create the pattern from Claim 26.1, but such a pattern is impossible, so we have a contradiction.

We can now think of the collection of black squares as a set of trees,
where each tree is formed by diagonally adjacent black squares.**Claim 26.3.** In any tree of black squares, there are at most two squares in the tree adjacent to the edge of the grid. Furthermore, if a tree has two squares adjacent to the edge of the grid, they must be on opposite edges (neither square can be a corner). Additionally, moving along any path connecting the top and bottom edges, starting from the bottom, one can only make up-left or up-right moves.

*Proof.* Consider any black square on the bottom edge of the grid. Suppose it is not a tree by itself; that is, it is diagonally connected to at least one other black square. WLOG let this black square be to the top right of it.

  [asy]
      size(5cm);
      path sq = (0,0)--(0,1)--(1,1)--(1,0)--cycle;
      fill(shift((0,0))*sq);
      fill(shift((1,1))*sq);
      fill(shift((2,2))*sq);
      fill(shift((3,3))*sq);
      fill(shift((4,2))*sq);
      fill(shift((1,-1))*sq,gray);
      for (int i=0;i<6;++i) {
      draw((i,0)--(i,6));
      draw((-1,i)--(6,i));
      }
    [/asy]
This pattern (with long side of any length) is impossible for the same reasons the pattern in 26.1 is impossible. Thus, moving along the path, starting from the gray square, we must alternate between left and right turns, so all moves must be up-left or up-right. Thus, no path may connect the bottom edge to itself.

  [asy]
      size(5cm);
      path sq = (0,0)--(0,1)--(1,1)--(1,0)--cycle;
      fill(shift((1,0))*sq);
      fill(shift((2,1))*sq);
      fill(shift((3,2))*sq);
      fill(shift((4,3))*sq);
      fill(shift((3,4))*sq);
      fill(shift((4,5))*sq);
      fill(shift((5,6))*sq);
      fill(shift((2,-1))*sq,gray);
      fill(shift((6,5))*sq,gray);
      for (int i=0;i<7;++i) {
      draw((i,0)--(i,7));
      draw((-1,i)--(6,i));
      }
    [/asy]

If any path connects the bottom edge to the right, like shown above, the implicit gray squares outside of the boundary force there to be a downward turn at the end, which creates an impossible configuration. Thus, any path connecting two edges must connect two opposite edges, and it readily follows that no tree can connect more than two edges, which proves our claim.**Claim 26.4.** This pattern is impossible.
  [asy]
      size(3cm);
      path sq = (0,0)--(0,1)--(1,1)--(1,0)--cycle;
      fill(shift((0,0))*sq);
      fill(shift((2,0))*sq);
      fill(shift((2,2))*sq);
      for (int i=0;i<4;++i) {
      draw((i,0)--(i,4));
      draw((-1,i)--(3,i));
      }
    [/asy]

*Proof.* Obvious.

Define a *vertex* as a point where four squares on the grid meet. Then there are  $(m-1)(n-1)$  vertices total. Furthermore, define the *count* of a configuration of black squares as the number of vertices adjacent to some black square, and define the *score* of a configuration as three times the number of squares minus the count.

Suppose there are  $c$  black squares. The maximum count is  $(m-1)(n-1)$ , so if we can prove the score is at most  $m+n-1$ , then the score equals  $3c-(m-1)(n-1)\le m+n-1$ , so  $3c\le mn$ . Thus, it suffices to prove that the score can never exceed  $m+n-1$ .**Claim 26.5.** If either dimension is  $2$ , the problem is true.

*Proof.* If  $m=2$ , then we induct. The problem is clear for  $n=1$ ,  $2$ ,  $3$ . If  $n\ge 4$ , if the leftmost column (of size  $2$ ) has no black squares, we trivially reduce to  $n-1$  or  $n-2$ . Otherwise, suppose that the top left square is black. Checkerboard color the board red and blue such that the top left square is red. Obviously, the second-leftmost square is blue, and let it be on column  $r$ . Then removing the leftmost  $2\times r$  rectangle reduces to  $n-r$ , as  $r\ge 3$ .

Now we suppose  $m$ ,  $n\ge 3$ .**Claim 26.6.** If there are no paths connecting two edges, then the score cannot exceed  $m+n-1$ .

*Proof.* If there are no paths connecting two edges, then each tree of black squares has at most one square on an edge. If a tree with one square on an edge has  $t$  squares, then the tree has  $t-1$  edges, so there are  $t-1$  vertices belonging to two of the squares. There are  $4$  vertices per square except for one, which has  $2$ , so the total count contributed by this tree is  $4t-(t-1)-2=3t-1$ . Thus, the score contributed by this tree is  $1$ . Similarly, the score contributed by a tree with a corner square is  $2$ , and the score contributed by a tree with no edge squares is  $-1$ .

Thus, the score is at most  $2a+b$ , where  $a$  is the number of black corners and  $b$  is the number of black edges. The number of squares in the perimeter of the grid is  $2m+2n-4$ . Also, note that if there are  $\frac{mn+1}{3}$  black squares, then the number of white squares is odd. Thus we only have to prove that the score is at most  $m+n$  (rather than  $m+n-1$ ).

Suppose  $m\ne 4$  and  $n\ne 4$ . Then consider the  $2m+2n$  unit segments along the perimeter of the grid. The number of black segments is greater than or equal to the score, and with the exception of the corner pairs, no pair of black segments can be adjacent. Additionally, Claim 26.4 gives that for each corner pair, if it is present, you can shift one of the black segments one unit over so that it is not adjacent to any black segment. As both dimensions are greater than  $5$ , no pair of shifted black segments can be adjacent either. Thus there are at most  $m+n$  black segments, so our claim is true.

If either dimension is  $4$ , WLOG  $m=4$  and  $n\ge 4$ .

[asy]
      size(4cm);
      path sq = (0,0)--(0,1)--(1,1)--(1,0)--cycle;
      fill(shift((0,0))*sq);
      fill(shift((2,0))*sq);
      fill(shift((0,3))*sq);
      fill(shift((2,3))*sq);
      dot((1,2));
      dot((2,2));
      for (int i=0;i<5;++i) {
      draw((i,0)--(i,4));
      draw((0,i)--(5,i));
      }
[/asy]

Looking at the black segments again, the only way to achieve a score of at least  $m+n+1$  is if this configuration appears on both sides, giving exactly  $m+n+1$ . This gives  $n\ge 5$ . Additionally, the dotted vertices cannot be a vertex of any black square, so the maximum count is  $(m-1)(n-1)-4=3n-7$ . Thus, if we have at least  $\frac{mn+2}{3}$  black squares, the score is at least  $4n+2-3n+7=n+9$ , which is impossible, so our claim is proven.

Now suppose that there exists at least one path connecting two edges. Observe that any path from bottom to top blocks all paths from left to right from forming, so WLOG all paths connecting two edges connect the top and bottom edges. Let there be  $k\ge 1$  such paths. Then there are  $k$  black squares on the top and bottom rows that correspond to each path.**Claim 26.7.** Every black square on one of the paths has a domino horizontally placed
  both to the left and to the right of it.

*Proof.* We will prove that each black square in a path has a horizontal domino to its left. Proving to the right is identical.

Consider any black square in the path such that the two (or one, if it is on the edge) squares next to it in the path are to its left. Call such a black square a \emph{valley}. Similarly, define a \emph{peak} to be a square such that both squares in the path next to it are to its right.

Consider any sequence of squares from a valley to a peak. The valley clearly must have a horizontal domino to its left, as does the black square diagonally adjacent to it. This process continues until the neighbor of the peak, so every black square that is not a peak has a horizontal domino to its left. As every peak is bordered by non-peaks, this must be true for peaks as well, proving our claim.

Consider the cells on the top row that are to the left of the leftmost top-bottom path or to the right of the rightmost top-bottom path. Call these cells *peripheral*, and define *left-peripheral* and *right-peripheral* in the obvious way. Every pair of cells in the top row must have at least  $2$  squares between them, so there are at least  $3k-2$  non-peripheral cells. If there are  $m$  rows and  $n$  columns, there are at most  $n-3k+2$  peripheral cells. Define these terms on the bottom row as well.

Let there be  $a$  and  $b$  left-peripheral cells on the top and bottom rows and  $c$  and  $d$  right-peripheral cells on the top and bottom rows respectively. Then  $a+b+c+d\le 2n-6k+4$ .**Claim 26.8.**
  If the score of a configuration with at least one top-bottom path
  exceeds  $m+n-1$ , then  $m=4$ .


*Proof.* Suppose  $m\ne 4$ . Consider the portion of the outer border of the grid that is left of the leftmost top-bottom path. This forms a chain of  $m+a+b-2$  orthogonally adjacent cells. Like before, any tree with a square on the edge but not the corner has score  $1$ , any tree with a square in a corner has score  $2$ , and any tree with no squares on the edge has score  $-1$ . Additionally, it is easy to see that a top-bottom tree has score  $3$ . We will prove that the score of everything left of the leftmost top-bottom path is at most  $\lceil\frac{m+a+b-4}{2}\rceil$ .

Consider the  $m+a+b$  segments on the outer border of the grid left of the leftmost top-bottom path. Every pair of these black segments, excluding corners, is nonconsecutive,  and Claim 26.4 gives that we can shift one of the corner segments for each corner such that the shifted segment is not adjacent to any other black segments. Also, by Claim 26.7, the two rightmost segments in the top row is not black, and neither are the two rightmost segments in the bottom row, so we have  $m+a+b-4$  segments to choose from such that no two are consecutive. The maximum number of segments is thus  $\lceil\frac{m+a+b-4}{2}\rceil$ . The number of black segments is greater than or equal to the total score left of the leftmost top-bottom path, so the score left of the leftmost top-bottom path is at most  $\lceil\frac{m+a+b-4}{2}\rceil$ .

Thus the total score is at most \[3k+\left\lceil\frac{m+a+b-4}{2}\right\rceil+\left\lceil\frac{m+c+d-4}{2}\right\rceil,\]
which is greater than or equal to  $3k+m+\frac{a+b+c+d}{2}-3$ , which is at most  $3k+m+n-3k+2-3=m+n-1$ . This proves our claim.

Now, if  $m=4$ , the only way to achieve more than  $\lceil\frac{m+a+b-4}{2}\rceil=\lceil\frac{a+b}{2}\rceil$  black segments is with this configuration:

[asy]
    size(4cm);
    path sq = (0,0)--(0,1)--(1,1)--(1,0)--cycle;
    fill(shift((0,0))*sq);
    fill(shift((2,0))*sq);
    fill(shift((0,3))*sq);
    fill(shift((2,3))*sq);
    for (int i=0;i<5;++i) {
    draw((i,0)--(i,4));
    draw((0,i)--(5,i));
    }
[/asy]

However, note that the rightmost squares on the top and bottom rows to the left of the leftmost top-bottom path are of the same color under a checkerboard coloring, so  $a+b$  is odd and therefore the maximum number of black segments is  $2+\lceil\frac{a-2}{2}\rceil+\lceil\frac{b-2}{2}\rceil=\lceil\frac{a+b}{2}\rceil$ . Thus  $m=4$  works for the same reasons as  $m\ne 4$ , so we are done.

