[*] Prove that if  $\omega$  passes through  $H$ , then lines  $PD$  and  $QE$  meet on  $\omega$  as well.
[/list]
*Merlijn Staps*</blockquote>
Let  $L, A'$  be the antipode of  $A$  in  $(ADE), \Omega$  respectively. Clearly,  $AI$  is the angle bisector of  $\angle BAC$  and so it meets  $\Omega$  at the arc midpoint  $M.$  Also define  $T$  be the second intersection of  $\Omega, (ADE).$  Then  $T, E, P$  and so are  $T, D, Q.$  Let  $K$  be the reflection of  $H$  over  $AL.$  We claim that  $K=QE \cap DP.$  By symmetry, it suffices to show that  $K,Q,E$  are collinear. We do this by making small claims:

<span style="color:#f00">**Claim 1:**</span>  $T, L, A'$  are collinear.
<span style="color:#00f">Proof:</span> This follows since  $\measuredangle ATL=\pi/2=\measuredangle ATA'.$   $\square$ <span style="color:#f00">**Claim 2:**</span>  $MP$  is perpendicular to  $AC.$  
<span style="color:#00f">Proof:</span> Clearly  $DE \parallel BP.$  Further,  $\measuredangle DEL=\measuredangle DAL= \measuredangle BAM=\measuredangle BPM$  and so we get  $MP \parallel LE \perp AC.$   $\square$ <span style="color:#f00">**Claim 3:**</span> We have  $\measuredangle PAC=\measuredangle QAB.$  Further,  $AP=AQ.$ <span style="color:#00f">Proof:</span> We can see that there is a spiral similarity at  $A$  taking  $DE \to QP$  by looking at  $\Omega, (AEF)$  intersecting in  $T,A.$   So we have the results.  $\square$ We want to show that  $E,K,Q$  are collinear. Reflecting about  $AM,$  this is equivalent to showing  $D,H,Q'$  are collinear, where  $Q'$  is the reflection of  $Q$  in  $AM.$  Now  $\measuredangle PAC=\measuredangle QAB=\measuredangle CAQ'$  and  $AP=AQ=AQ'$  imply that  $Q'$  is the reflection of  $P$  over  $AC.$ [asy]
 /* Geogebra to Asymptote conversion, documentation at artofproblemsolving.com/Wiki go to User:Azjps/geogebra */
import graph; size(12cm); 
real labelscalefactor = 0.5; /* changes label-to-point distance */
