Thus, reflecting the current situation over  $AC$  we want to prove that  $P, H', D'$  are collinear, where  $H',D'$  are the reflections of  $H,D$  in  $AC.$  Notice that  $H' \in \Omega.$  So we have
\begin{align*}
\measuredangle PH'H &= \measuredangle PAB 
&= \measuredangle CAB+\measuredangle PAC 
&= \measuredangle A+(\measuredangle PTA'-\measuredangle CTA') 
&= \measuredangle A+(\measuredangle ETL-\measuredangle CAA') 
&=\measuredangle A+(\measuredangle LAD-\measuredangle HAB) 
&=\measuredangle A+\measuredangle LAH 
&=\measuredangle CAB+\pi/2-\measuredangle HLA 
&=(\pi/2-\measuredangle BAC)-\measuredangle HDB 
&=\measuredangle HBD+\measuredangle BDH 
&=\measuredangle H'HD 
&= \measuredangle D'H'H
\end{align*}
and so we are done.  $\blacksquare$

