(a) Let  $L$  be the arc midpoint of  $BC$ . Note that  $\widehat{QA}+\widehat{LC}=\pi$  and  $\widehat{PA}+\widehat{LB}+\pi$ , so  $\widehat{QA}=\widehat{PA}$  and  $\widehat{PQ}=\widehat{BC}$ .

This implies that  $\triangle ADE\sim\triangle AQP$ , so there is a spiral similarity at  $A$  sending  $DE$  to  $QP$ . Defining  $T=QD\cap PE$ , this means that  $A=(TDE)\cap(TQP)$ , so  $T=(ADE)\cap(ABC)$ , which is on  $(ADE)$ , as desired.

(b) Let  $S=QE\cap PD$ . We first claim that  $Q,E,E'$  and  $P,D,D'$  are collinear, where  $E'$  is the reflection of  $H$  over  $AC$  and  $D'$  is the reflection of  $H$  over  $AB$ . To see this, note that
\[\angle EE'A=\angle AHE=\angle ADE=\frac{1}{2}\widehat{AQ}=\angle AE'Q.\]
We also have that
\[\widehat{AE'}+\widehat{PQ}=2\angle ACE'+2\angle BAC=2(\angle ACH+\angle BAC)=\pi,\]
so  $QE'\perp AP$ , and similarly  $PD'\perp AQ$ . Thus,  $S$  is the orthocenter of  $APQ$ .

We'll now show that  $S$  lies on  $(ADE)$ . Note that  $BQCP$  is an isosceles trapezoid, so the directions  $BC$  and  $QP$  are reflections in  $AL$ , so  $AH$  and  $AS$  are reflections in  $AL$ . Furthermore, we have that
\[AS=2R\cos\angle QAP=2R\cos\angle BAC=AH,\]
so  $S$  is the reflection of  $H$  in  $AL$ , which lies on  $(ADE)$ , as desired.

[list=a]
[*] Define  $X\equiv (ABC)\cap (ADE)\neq A$ . Reim's theorem trivially implies that  $X$  lies on both  $\overline{PE}$  and  $\overline{QD}$ . Notice that, as an extra consequence,  $\triangle ADE\sim\triangle AQP$ . This will be used in the next portion.


[*] Let  $H'$  be the orthocenter of triangle  $APQ$ : we first claim that if  $H$  lies on  $\omega$ , then  $H'$  must also lie on  $\omega$ . To show this, first note that  $\overline{AH'}$  must pass through the circumcenter of  $\triangle ABC$ , so  $\angle BAH = \angle CAH'$  and  $\angle BAH' = \angle CAH$ . This, in conjunction with  $$ AH = \frac{BC}{\tan \angle A} = \frac{QP}{\tan\angle QAP} = AH' $$  and  $AD = AE$  implies that  $\triangle ADH\cong \triangle AEH'$  and  $\triangle ADH'\cong\triangle AEH$ . Hence,  $ADHE\cong AEH'D$ , and our desired conclusion immediately follows.
