Let  $X$  be the other intersection of  $(ADE)$  and  $(ABC)$ . We claim that  $PE$  and  $QD$  both pass through  $X$ , proving part (a).**<span style="color:#f00">Claim.**</span> A spiral similarity at  $X$  takes  $XDE$  to  $XBC$ .

*Proof.* This is quite well known (and has appeared in many problems before, a recent one being 2016 G2), and is just angle chasing:   $$ \measuredangle DXE = \measuredangle DAE = \measuredangle BAC = \measuredangle BXC, $$   while   $$ \measuredangle XDE = \measuredangle XAE = \measuredangle XBC. \ \blacksquare $$  Now it suffices to show that if  $XD$  intersects  $(ABC)$  at  $Q'$  and similarly define  $P'$ , then  $\overline{CQ'} \parallel \overline{BP'} \parallel \overline{DE}$ . The spiral similarity gives us   $$ \measuredangle XDE = \measuredangle XBC = \measuredangle XQC, $$   which proves the claim, finishing the first part.  $\square$ Now for part (b). This time, we claim that  $PD$  and  $QE$  meet at  $F$ , the intersection of the diameter of  $(ABC)$  through  $A$  and  $(ADE)$ . Again define  $P'$  to be the intersection of ray  $\overrightarrow{DF}$  with  $(ABC)$  and  $Q'$  similarly. It suffices to show that  $\overline{BP'} \parallel \overline{CQ'} \parallel \overline{DE}$ , as before.

Let  $I$  be the  $A$ -antipode in  $(ADE)$ . We claim that  $D, E$  and  $H, F$  are symmetric around  $AI$ . The first part is obvious because  $AD=AE$ , while the second part follows because  $\overline{AH}$  and  $\overline{AF}$  are isogonal in  $\triangle ABC$ , while  $H$  and  $F$  both lie on  $(ADE)$  by definition.

Our final claim is the following:**<span style="color:#f00">Claim.**</span>  $Q'F$  meets  $(ABC)$  at  $H_B \neq Q'$ , the reflection of  $H$  over  $AC$ , while  $P'F$  meets  $(ABC)$  at  $H_C \neq P'$ , the reflection of  $H$  over  $AB$ .

*Proof.* Define  $H_B, H_C$  as shown above. By symmetry, it suffices to show  $Q', F, H_B$  are collinear. This is just angle chasing:   $$ \measuredangle AEH_B = \measuredangle HEA = \measuredangle HFA = \measuredangle AHF = \measuredangle AEF $$   because  $AH=AF$ , proving the claim.  $\blacksquare$ To finish, notice that   $$ \measuredangle DEQ' = \measuredangle DEF = \measuredangle DAF = \measuredangle HAE = \measuredangle H_A AC = \angle H_BQ'C = \measuredangle EQ'C, $$   because it is well-known that  $C$  is the arc midpoint of arc  $H_AH_B$ . This proves  $\overline{CQ'} \parallel \overline{DE}$ , finishing the problem.  $\square$

For the part (a) pure angle chasing. Let  $\Omega$  and  $\omega$  intersect at  $F\neq A$ . Now,
\begin{eqnarray*}
\angle AFE=\angle ADE=\angle ABP=\angle AFP.
\end{eqnarray*}
