We make no synthetic observations.

If  $K'$  varies on a fixed line through  $A$ , the line formed by the intersections of the circle through  $A$  centered at  $K'$  with  $\overline{AB}$  and  $\overline{AC}$  is parallel to a fixed line, hence there is exactly one choice of  $K'$  that makes this line pass through  $H$ . Therefore, it will suffice to take  $D$  as a variable point on  $(ABC)$ , define  $T$  as the point on  $(ABC)$  such that  $\overline{BC} \perp \overline{DT}$ , and show that  $\overline{AD} \cap \overline{TH}$  is this point.

Use complex numbers with  $(ABC)$  as the unit circle, letting  $X=x$ , etc. Then  $T=-\tfrac{bc}{d}$ , and the second intersection of  $\overline{TH}$  with  $(ABC)$  is  $$ S:=\frac{-\frac{bc}{d}-a-b-c}{\frac{bc}{d}(\frac{1}{a}+\frac{1}{b}+\frac{1}{c})-1}-\frac{ad(a+b+c)+abc}{ab+ac+bc+ad}. $$  We now compute  $$ \overline{AD} \cap \overline{TS}=\frac{ad(-\frac{bc}{d}+\frac{abc+ad(a+b+c)}{ab+ac+bc+ad})+\frac{bc(abc+ad(a+b+c))}{d(ab+ac+bc+ad)}(a+d)}{ad+\frac{bc(abc+ad(a+b+c))}{t(ab+ac+bc+ad)}}=\frac{ad(abcd+ad^2(a+b+c)-bc(ab+ac+bc+ad))+bc(abc+ad(a+b+c))(a+d)}{ad^2(ab+ac+bc+ad)+bc(abc+ad(a+b+c))}. $$  The denominator factors as  $a(b+d)(c+d)(ad+bc)$ , and the numerator factors as  $a(ad+bc)((a+b+c)d^2+abc)$ , hence this expression actually equals  $$ z:=\frac{(a+b+c)d^2+abc}{(b+d)(c+d)} \implies \overline{z}=\frac{(\frac{1}{a}+\frac{1}{b}+\frac{1}{c})d^2+\frac{1}{abc}}{(\frac{1}{b}+\frac{1}{d})(\frac{1}{c}+\frac{1}{d})}=\frac{ab+ac+bc+d^2}{a(b+d)(c+d)}. $$  Note that  $ad+bc=0$  is handled by continuity.

It suffices to show that the midpoint of  $\overline{AH}$  is collinear with the feet from  $z$  to  $\overline{AB}$  and  $\overline{AC}$ . This is the same as saying  $a+\tfrac{1}{2}(b+c),\tfrac{1}{2}(a+b+z-ab\overline{z}),\tfrac{1}{2}(a+c+z-ac\overline{z})$  collinear, which by shifting and scaling is equivalent to  $a+b+c,b+z-ab\overline{z},c+z-ac\overline{z}$  collinear. We now compute
\begin{align*}
(a+b+c)-(c+z-ac\overline{z})&=a+b-\frac{(a+b+c)d^2+abc}{(b+d)(c+d)}+ac\frac{ab+ac+bc+d^2}{a(b+d)(c+d)}
&=\frac{(a+b)(bc+bd+cd+d^2)-ad^2-bd^2-cd^2-abc+abc+ac^2+bc^2+cd^2}{(b+d)(c+d)}
&=\frac{(a+b)(bc+bd+cd+d^2-d^2+c^2)}{(b+d)(c+d)}=\frac{(a+b)(b+c)(c+d)}{(b+d)(c+d)}.
\end{align*}
From a symmetric computation it follows that  $$ \frac{(a+b+c)-(c+z-ac\overline{z})}{(a+b+c)-(b+z-ab\overline{z})}=\frac{(a+b)(b+c)(c+d)}{(a+c)(b+c)(b+d)}=\frac{a+b}{a+c} \div \frac{d+b}{d+c}, $$  which is real since  $a,d,-b,-c$  are all concyclic on the unit circle.  $\blacksquare$ **Remark:** By far the hardest step in this problem is factoring our expression for  $\overline{AD} \cap \overline{TS}$ : the second step is comparatively more straightforward and only requires inspection. When doing the problem, I sought to find factors of the denominator—factors of the numerator are difficult because there doesn't appear to be a simple condition for  $Z$  to be the circumcenter of  $\triangle ABC$ . However, I noticed that when  $D$  is either the  $B$  or  $C$  antipode, we have  $T=C,B$  (respectively) and thus  $\overline{AD} \parallel \overline{TH}$ , so in fact  $(b+d)(c+d)$  must factor the denominator. Pulling these factors out makes the rest easy, and now we have a factor of  $ad+bc$  as well. However, if  $D$  is chosen very close to the second intersection of the  $A$ -altitude with  $(ABC)$ , it doesn't actually appear that  $Z$  goes to infinity, so we must have a factor of  $ad+bc$  in the numerator as well. This ends up giving us our extremely nice expression for  $z$ .

