Here was the original solution I found, with some suggestions from Evan to make it simpler.

<details><summary>Solution</summary>It is sufficient to find a single point  $P$  satisfying both conditions. We construct such a point as follows. Since  $AD^2 + BC^2 = AB^2$ , there exists a point  $P$  on  $\overline{AB}$  satisfying
\[AD^2 = AP \cdot AB \quad \text{and} \quad BC^2 = BP \cdot BA.\]
Thus  $\triangle APD \stackrel{-}{\sim} \triangle ADB$  and  $\triangle BPC \stackrel{-}{\sim} \triangle BCA$ , so
\[\measuredangle APD = \measuredangle BDA = \measuredangle BCA = \measuredangle CPB\]
as desired.

Now we prove that line  $PE$  bisects  $\overline{CD}$ . Define  $K = \overline{AC} \cap \overline{PD}$  and  $L = \overline{BD} \cap \overline{PC}$ .
[asy]
    import olympiad;
    import cse5;
    unitsize(100);
    pair A, B, C, D, O, E, P, M, K, L, X, Y;
    pair Xa, Xb;
    A = point(unitcircle, intersections(unitcircle, (1, -0.4), (-1, -0.4))[0]);
    B = scale(-1, 1) * A;
    P = A + 0.43 * (B - A);
    O = origin;
    Xa = foot(P, A, O);
    Xb = foot(P, B, O);
    C = point(unitcircle, intersections(unitcircle, P, Xb)[0]);
    D = point(unitcircle, intersections(unitcircle, P, Xa)[0]);
    E = extension(A, C, B, D);
    M = (C+D)/2;
    K = extension(A, C, P, D);
    L = extension(B, D, P, C);
    X = 2*foot(O, K, P) - D;
    Y = 2*foot(O, L, P) - C;

