The answer is if  $a=b$  or  $a=-b$ . In the former case, one can take  $f(x) \equiv x+a$  and  $g(x) \equiv x$ . In the latter case, one can take  $f(x) \equiv -x+a$  and  $g(x) = -x$ .

Now we prove these are the only possibilities. First:**<span style="color:red">Claim:</span>**  	The functions  $f$  and  $g$  are bijections.

*Proof.*  	Surjectivity is obvious. 	To see injective, note that if  $f(u) = f(v)$  	then  $g(f(u)) = g(f(v)) \implies u+b = v+b \implies u=v$ , 	and similarly for  $g$ .  $\blacksquare$ Note also that for any  $x$ , we have \begin{align*} 	f(x+b) &= f(g(f(x))) = f(x) + a  	g(x+a) &= g(f(g(x))) = g(x) + b. \end{align*} If either  $a$  is zero or  $b$  is zero, we immediately get the other is zero, and hence done. So assume  $ab \ne 0$ .

If  $|b| > |a|$ , then two of \[ \{ f(0), f(1), \dots, f(b-1) \} \pmod a \] coincide, which together with repeatedly applying the first equation above will then give a contradiction to injectivity of  $f$ . Similarly, if  $|a| > |b|$  swapping the roles of  $f$  and  $g$  (and  $a$  and  $b$ ) will give a contradiction to injectivity of  $g$ . This completes the proof.


