The  $k$ -element set of labels  $X = \{x_1, x_2, \dots, x_k\}$  must be a subset of the  $k + 1$  -element set  $L = \{\ell_1, \ell_2, \dots, \ell_k, m + n + 1\}$ , so there is exactly one element, say  $\ell$  in  $L \setminus X$ . By considering the path with labels  $x_1, x_2, \dots, x_{k + 1}, y_{k + 1}$  we see that  $x_{k + 1}$  and  $y_{k + 1}$  are  $\ell$  and  $\ell_{k + 1}$  in some order, and either works. So there are  $2$  ways to choose the labels of each pair  $(x_i, y_i)$  and thus  $2^m$  ways to complete the labeling in total. This proves our formula and finishes the problem.

Here's a cute bijection:

Arrange the sets into the obvious  $(n+1) \times (n+1)$  square grid. Here, we orient the grid diagonally so that  $S_{0,0}$  is on the bottom and  $S_{n,n}$  is on the top. Label an internal edge with  $i$  if crossing an edge while going up adds the element  $i$  to the set in the relevant square.

It follows from the  $S_{i,j} \subseteq S_{k,\ell}$  condition that the set of edges with label  $i$  must follow a path from the left end of the square to the right end of the square, so we can associate every valid arrangement of sets with a partitioning of the internal edges into  $2n$  paths, labeled  $1,2,\ldots, 2n$ , travelling from the left end of the square to the right end of the square. Indeed, this correspondence is reversible, since given the system of labeled paths one can associate a square with the set of all numbers  $k$  so that the path labeled with  $k$  passes below the square.

It suffices to show that there are  $2^{n^2}$  ways to choose all the ways to paritition the unlabeled edges into  $2n$  *unlabeled* paths. However, since every edge is utilized, this is equivalent to, for each of the  $n^2$  internal vertices, choosing whether the two paths that pass through the vertex end up crossing or not. Thus, we are done.

