As you can see, arranging set like this give us a clear hierarchy of which set affect on which set. We now name each <u>column</u>(vertical) of sets. Column containing  $S_{44},S_{33},\dots,S_{00}$  we name it layer 1. Column containing  $S_{43},S_{32},\dots,S_{10}$  and column containing   $S_{34},S_{23},\dots,S_{01}$  we name it layer 2. Do the same until we reach column containing  $S_{40}$  and column containing  $S_{04}$  which is layer 5. For any  $n$ , we do this process.

Now we pick members in sets in layer 1 first. Since  $S_{22}$  must have all members from  $S_{11}$ , same with  $S_{33},S_{44}\dots,S_{nn}$ . 
We can pick  $\binom{2n}{2}\binom{2n-2}{2}\cdots\binom{2}{2} = \frac{(2n)!}{2!(2n-2)!}\cdot\frac{(2n-2)!}{2!(2n-4)!}\cdots\frac{2!}{2!(0)!} = \frac{(2n)!}{2^n}$  ways by the multiplication principle. Since we already choose members in layer 1, in layer 2, each sets can only pick one of the two members that are above. E.g. If  $S_{11} = \{1,2\}$  and  $S_{22} = \{1,2,3,4\}$ .  $S_{12}$  and  $S_{21}$  can only pick to have  $\{1,2,3\}$  or  $\{1,2,4\}$ , because  $S_{12}$  and  $S_{21}$  need to have all the members from  $S_{11}$  and need to have one additional members from  $S_{22}$ . Thus each sets in layer 2 have 2 ways of picking. We can see that there are  $2n$  sets in layer 2. Therefore, there are  $2^{2n}$  ways of picking. Using the same logic to layer 3,4,...,n+1. The product from multiplying all layers is  $2^{2n}\cdot2^{2(n-1)}\cdots2^{2} = 2^{n(n+1)}$ . Multiplying ways of picking members in layer 1 and other layers gives  $\frac{(2n)!}{2^n}\cdot2^{n^2+n} = (2n)!\cdot2^{n^2}$ .  $\blacksquare$

I claim that the answer is all positive evens. As a construction for an even  $a$ , consider the function \begin{align*}
f(x)=
\begin{cases}
  x \text{ for } x\neq a,1000 
  a \text{ for } x=1000 
  1000 \text { for } x=a 
\end{cases}
\end{align*}
Clearly, this function satisfies the assertion.

Now, suppose that  $f(1000)$  is odd. First, we will show injectivity. If  $f(a)=f(b)$ , then the condition implies  $a^2=b^2\implies a=b$  as desired. Now, consider the sequence  $S:\,1000,f(1000),f^2(1000),\ldots$ . Note that, if we plug  $f^k(1000)$  into the assertion, we get  $f^{f^{k+1}(1000)+k}(1000)f^{k+2}(1000)=f^k(1000)^2$ . So, one of the factors on the LHS is  $\le f^k(1000)$ . In particular, this implies that for all elements in  $S$ , there always exists a later element which is at most the current number. However, we can't have it always strictly decreasing, since all the values are positive integers, so eventually we find  $i>j$  such that  $f^i(1000)=f^j(1000)$ , which combined with injectivity gives that  $f^{i-j}(1000)=1000$ . Therefore,  $S$  is periodic with period  $i-j$ .

Now, suppose we have an odd  $x$ . By the condition,  $f^2(x)|x^2$ , so  $f^2(x)$  is odd. This means that  $f^3(1000),f^5(1000),$  etc. are all odd. In general,  $f^{2i+1}(1000)\equiv 1\pmod 2$ . Now, plug  $1000$  into the assertion to get that  $1000^2=f^2(1000)f^{f(1000)}(1000)$ . The latter factor is odd, so we must have  $2^6|f^2(1000)$ . Similarly, after plugging in  $f^2(1000)$ , we realize that  $2^{12}|f^4(1000)$ , and then  $2^{24}|f^6(1000)$ , etc. So, the sequence  $\{v_2(f^{2i}(1000)\}$  is unbounded, which of course implies that  $S$  is unbounded, as all terms are nonzero. However, this is a contradiction to the fact that it is periodic, and therefore  $f(1000)$  cannot be odd.

The answer is that  $f(1000)$  can equal  $2k$  for any positive integer  $k$ . This indeed works; consider the construction   $$ f(n)=
\begin{cases}
n & \text{ if } n \not\in \{1000,2k \} 
1000+2k-n & \text{ if } n \in \{1000, 2k \} 
\end{cases} $$  . Now we show  $f(1000)$  cannot be odd. First note that  $f(n)$  is injective, since if  $f(a)=f(b)$  then we get  $\frac{a^2}{f(f(a))}=\frac{b^2}{f(f(b))}$ . Thus it suffices to show that for all odd  $n$  we have  $f(n)=n$ . Let  $P(n)$  denote the given functional equation. We strong induct on  $n$ .

Base Case:  $f(1)=1$ Proof: Take  $P(1)$ ; then since both sides are integers we must have  $f(f(1))=1$ . Let  $f(1)=k$  so  $f(k)=1$ . Then taking  $P(k)$  we get  $1=f(k)=\frac{k^2}{f(f(k))}=k$  as claimed.

Now assume  $f(k)=k$  for all odd  $k<n$ . Take  $P(n)$  to get  $[f^{f(n)}(n)][f(f(n))]=n^2$ ; by Claim 1 and injectivity, neither of the bracketed factors on the left hand side can equal an odd integer less than  $n$ . Thus we must have  $f(f(n))=n$ ; let  $f(n)=m$  so  $f(m)=n$ . Then taking  $P(m)$  we get  $n=f^n(m)=\frac{m^2}{f(f(m))}=m$  as claimed.

The answer is all  $P(x) = C(x^2+3)$ . Rewrite the equation to be \begin{align*} \frac{xP(x) + yP(y) + zP(z)}{xyz} = P(x-y)+P(y-z)+P(z-x)\end{align*}**<span style="color:#f00">Claim:</span>**  $P$  is even

*Proof.* We multiply both sides of the equation by  $xyz$  and note that the new equation must also hold if  $x,y,z$  is not nonzero. Subbing in  $z = 0$ , we have that  $xP(x) + yP(y) = 0$  for all  $x = -y$ , implying that  $P$  is even.  $\square$ Let  $(x, y, z) = \left(t, \tfrac{1}{t}, t+\tfrac{1}{t} \right)$ . Subbing this into the given assertion and cancelling out terms, we have \begin{align*} \left(t+\tfrac{1}{t} \right) \left(P\left(t+\tfrac{1}{t}\right) - P\left(t-\tfrac{1}{t} \right) \right) = \tfrac{1}{t} P(t) + tP(\tfrac{1}{t}) ~~~~~(\clubsuit) \end{align*}

<span style="color:#f00">**Claim:**</span>  $\operatorname{deg} P \le 2$ *Proof.* For contradiction, assume  $P$  has leading term  $a_nx^{2n}$  where  $n > 1$ . Using  $(\clubsuit)$ , we have that the leading term on the left-hand side is  $4n a_n t^{2n-1}$  while the leading term on the right-hand side is  $a_n t^{2n-1}$ , implying that  $a_n = 0$ .  $\square$ A quick check of  $ax^2+b$  shows that  $b = 3a$  is required, which gives our solution set. To show that these indeed work, we only need to prove that  $x^2+3$  works due to scaling. Plugging everything in, we get \begin{align*} \frac{x^3+y^3+z^3 + 3(x+y+z)}{xyz} - 9 = 2(x^2+y^2+z^2-xy-yz-xz) \end{align*} which is true by the  $x^3+y^3+z^3-3xyz$  factorization.  $\blacksquare$

