\mathbb E[x_{n+1}^\frac1m] = \frac{\sum_{i=0}^{\left \lceil 2.01x_n \right \rceil} i^\frac 1m}{\left \lceil 2.01x_n \right \rceil+1} &< \frac1{\left \lceil 2.01x_n \right \rceil+1} \left(1+\int_0^{\left \lceil 2.01x_n \right \rceil} x^\frac 1m dx \right) 
=\frac{1}{\left \lceil 2.01x_n \right \rceil+1} \left(1+\frac m{m+1} \left \lceil 2.01x_n \right \rceil^{\frac{m+1}m} \right) 
< \frac m{m+1} 2.71^\frac 1m x_n^{\frac 1m}
\end{align*} So taking  $\mathbb{E}$  again both sides we get that \[\frac{\mathbb{E}[x_{n+1}^\frac 1m]}{\mathbb{E}[x_n^\frac 1m]} < \frac m{m+1} 2.71^\frac 1m\] Now multiplying all the fractions together and taking the limit, we have \begin{align*}
\lim_{n \to \infty} \mathbb{E}[x_n^\frac 1m] & <\lim_{n \to \infty} 2019^\frac 1m \left(\frac{m}{m+1} 2.71^\frac 1m \right)^n 
