Basically, note that by the law of sines,  $$ \dfrac{\sin(A)}{a}=\dfrac{\sin(B)}{b}=\dfrac{\sin(C)}{c}=\dfrac{1}{2R}. $$  So all side lengths are proportional to the sines of their corresponding angles by a factor of  $2R$ . Since  $2R$  can be whatever we want it to be, we can use the ratios of the sines to get the ratio of the sides, from which it is easy to get an answer.

Edit: actually, here's a full <details><summary>Solution.</summary>By the law of sines, note that  $$ \frac{\sin(A)}{a}=\frac{\sin(B)}{b}=\frac{\sin(C)}{c}=\frac{1}{2R}. $$  This means that the ratio of the side lengths is equal to the ratio of the sines of the corresponding angles. Since we can scale the triangle however we want, we can make  $R$  any value, thus scaling the sides by any factor of the sines. We now can compute the sines.
\begin{align*}
\sin(A)&=\sqrt{1-\cos^2(A)}=\tfrac{3\sqrt{15}}{16}
\sin(B)&=\sqrt{1-\cos^2(B)}=\tfrac{\sqrt{15}}{8}
\sin(C)&=\sqrt{1-\cos^2(C)}=\tfrac{\sqrt{15}}{4}
\end{align*}It's not hard to see that these are in a ratio of  $3:2:4$ ; and since we can let  $R$  be whatever we want it to be, this is also the smallest set of integer sides, leading to a least perimeter of  $\boxed{9}.$</details>

