Often times, log problems simply require the basic log base change identity. This problem is no exception
\begin{align*}
a_n &= (n\, \heartsuit\, (n-1)) \,\diamondsuit\, a_{n-1}
&= \left(n^{ \frac{1}{\log_{7}{n-1}} }\right)^{ \log_{7}{a_{n-1}} }
&= n^{\frac{\log_{7}{a_{n-1}}}{ \log_{7}{n-1}  } }
&= n^{ \log_{n-1}{a_{n-1}} }
\end{align*}
Evaluate terms
\begin{align*}
a_3 &= 3^{\frac{1}{ \log_{7}{2} }}
a_4 &= 4^{ \log_{3}{3^{\frac{1}{ \log_{7}{2} }}} } = 4^{\frac{1}{ \log_{7}{2} } }
\cdots
a_n &= n^{\frac{1}{ \log_{7}{2} } }
\end{align*}
\[ \log_{7}{a_{2019}} = \log_{7}{ 2019^ {\frac{1}{\log_{7}{2}}}    }   = \frac{ \log_{7}{2019}  }{ \log_{7}{2} } = \log_{2}{2019} \approx \boxed{11}  \]

