<details><summary>Ceva and Trig</summary>We easily see that the triangle is isoceles, with  $\angle A = \angle B$ .
We also have that  $EA^2 = EF.EC$ ,  $CD.CB=CF.CE$ . By Ceva we want to show that;  $$ \frac{EH}{HB}=\frac{EA^2}{BD.BC} $$  where  $H = \overline{EB} \cap \overline{AC}$ .
But since the triangle is isoceles we have that:  $$ \frac{EH}{HB}=\frac{EA}{AB} $$  this holds since  $\angle EAC = \angle CAB$ .
Now we want to show that:  $$ EA.AB=BD.BC $$  which translates into:  $$ \frac{\sin (180-2\angle A)}{\sin (\angle A)}=\frac{BD}{EA} $$  But this holds since notice that by a angle-chase we have that  $\angle AED = 180-2\angle A$ , just look at the triangle  $\triangle AED$  and also notice that  $\angle BAD = \angle ADE$ .
Now by the law of sines we have that  $BD=\frac{\sin \angle BAD}{\sin \angle A}AD$  and that  $EA=\frac{\sin \angle ADE}{\sin 180-2\angle A}AD$ From here we see that:  $$ \frac{\sin (180-2\angle A)}{\sin (\angle A)}=\frac{BD}{EA} $$  Now we deduce by Ceva on the triangle  $CEB$ , that the lines  $AC,BF,DE$  are all concurrent.</details>

