If I am not mistaken   $$ \min S= \begin{cases}
12, \ \ \text{if} \ n=3 \ (\text{when} \ a_i=b_i=2) 
16, \ \ \text{if} \ n \ge 4 \ (\text{for example,} \ a_1=a_2=a_3=a_4=4, \ a_i=0, \ \forall i \ge 5, \ b_i=0, \ \forall i \neq 2, \ b_2=16)
\end{cases} $$  For each  $n$ , let  $S_1 = a_1+a_3+ \cdots + a_{2n-1}$  and  $S_2=a_2+a_4+\cdots + a_{2n}$  and  $S$  be sum of  $a_1,a_2,\dots,a_{2n}.$  There are two cases ***Case 1.***  $ \ n=3$ We have  $a_1+a_2+\cdots + a_6 = b_1+b_2+ \cdots + b_6$  and  $a_i a_{i+2} \ge b_i+b_{i+1}$ , so
\begin{align*}
a_1a_3+a_3a_5+a_5a_1 &\ge \left(b_1+b_2 \right) + \left(b_3+b_4 \right) + \left(b_5+b_6 \right) = S, 
a_2a_4+a_4a_6+a_6a_2 &\ge \left(b_2+b_3 \right) + \left(b_4+b_5 \right) + \left(b_6+b_1 \right) = S.
\end{align*}
But the AM - GM inequality implies
\begin{align*}
S_1^2 \ge 3 \left(a_1a_3+a_3a_5+a_5a_1 \right) \ge 3S = 3 \left(S_1+S_2 \right), 
S_2^2 \ge 3 \left(a_2a_4+a_4a_6+a_6a_2 \right) \ge 3S = 3 \left(S_1+S_2 \right), 
\end{align*}
So,  $\min \left \{S_1,S_2 \right \} \ge 6, $  or  $S_1,S_2 \ge 6.$  Therefore  $S=S_1+S_2 \ge 12.$ ***Case 2.***  $ \ n \ge 4$ This case is similar to the previous. Notice that the inequality  $$ x_1x_2 + x_2x_3 + \cdots + x_{n-1}x_n+x_{n}x_{1} \le \frac{\left(x_1+x_2+\cdots + x_n \right)^2}{4}, $$   holds for all  $x_i \ge 0$  and  $n \ge 4.$ We have
\begin{align*}
S_1^2 & \ge 4 \left(\sum_{i=0}^{n-1} a_{2i+1}a_{2i+3} \right) \ge 4 \left(\sum_{i=0}^{n-1} \left(b_{2i+1}+b_{2i+2} \right) \right) = 4S 
S_2^2 & \ge 4 \left(\sum_{i=1}^{n} a_{2i}a_{2i+2} \right) \ge 4 \left(\sum_{i=1}^{n} \left(b_{2i}+b_{2i+1} \right) \right) = 4S 
\end{align*}
From this, we also get  $S_1,S_2 \ge 8$  and therefore  $S=S_1+S_2 \ge 16.$

