We are going to apply barycentric coordinates. Let  $ \triangle ABC $  be the reference triangle with  $ A = (1,0,0) , B =(0,1,0) $  and  $ C = (0,0,1) $ .**<span style="color:#f00">Lemma 1 : </span>** Let  $ M_a , M_b , M_c $   be midpoints of sides  $ BC, CA , AB $  respectively. Let  $ M_aM_b \cap IB = X_b $ . Then  $ X_bC \perp IB $ .

Let us compute  $X_b $ , as  $ X_b \in IB $ , let  $ X_b = (a : t : c )$ .
Now,  $ M_a , M_b , X_b $  are collinear. $ \therefore \begin{vmatrix} a & t & c  1 & 0 & 1  0 & 1 & 1 \end{vmatrix} = 0 $  $ \Rightarrow t = c-a $  $ \boxed{\therefore (CX_b)_{\infty} = ( a : c-a : -c ) }$ Now, as  $ IA_1 \perp IB \Rightarrow IA_1 \parallel CX_b $  $ \Rightarrow I , A_1 , (CX_b)_{\infty} $  are collinear. Let  $ A_1 = ( 0 : y : z ) $ , then $ \begin{vmatrix} 0 & y & z   a & b & c  a & c-a & -c \end{vmatrix} = 0 $ Applying  $ R_2 \to R_2 - R_3 $  we get $ \begin{vmatrix} 0 & y & z  0 & a+b-c & 2c  a  & c-a & -c \end{vmatrix} = 0 $  $\Rightarrow y : z = a+b-c : 2c = s-c : c $  $ \boxed{ \therefore A_1 = ( 0 : s-c : c )} $ Now  $ (ABC) := \sum_{cyc} a^2 yz = 0 $  $ A_3 \in AA_1 $  so let  $ A_3 = ( t : s-c : c ) $ , now as  $ A_3 \in (ABC ) \Rightarrow a^2(s-c)c+ b^2ct + c^2t(s-c) = 0 $  $ \Rightarrow a^2(s-c) + (b^2 +cs - c^2 )t = 0 $  $ \Rightarrow t = -\frac{a^2(s-c)}{b^2 + cs - c^2} $  $\boxed{ \therefore A_3 = ( -a^2(s-c) : (s-c)(b^2 +cs -c^2) : c(b^2 + cs - c^2 )) }$ Similarly,  $\boxed{ A_4 = ( -a^2(s-b) : b(c^2 + bs - b^2) : (s-b)(c^2 + bs - b^2) )} $  $ A_0 \in BC $ , let  $ A_0 = ( 0 : y : z ) $  and as  $ A_0 , A_3 , A_4 $  are collinear  $ \Rightarrow \begin{vmatrix} -a^2(s-c) & (s-c)(b^2 +cs -c^2 ) & c(b^2+ cs -c^2 )  -a^2(s-b) & b(c^2 + bs - b^2) & (s-b)(c^2 + bs - b^2 )  0 & y & z \end{vmatrix} = 0 $  $ \Rightarrow \begin{vmatrix} (s-c) & (s-c)(b^2 +cs -c^2 ) & c(b^2+ cs -c^2 )  (s-b) & b(c^2 + bs - b^2) & (s-b)(c^2 + bs - b^2 )  0 & y & z \end{vmatrix} = 0 $ Now applying  $ C_2 \to C_2 - (b^2 + cs -c^2 )C_1$  and  $ C_3 \to C_3 - (c^2+bs -c^2)C_1 $  $ \Rightarrow \begin{vmatrix} s-c & 0 & bs(s-a)  s-b & cs(s-a) & 0  0 & y & z \end{vmatrix} = 0 $  $ \Rightarrow \begin{vmatrix} s-c & 0 & b  s-b & c & 0  0 & y & z \end{vmatrix} = 0 $  $ \Rightarrow y : z = -c(s-c) : b(s-b) $  $ \boxed{\therefore A_0 = ( 0 : -c(s-c) : b(s-b) )} $ In terms of directed lengths we have  $ \frac{A_0B}{A_0C} = - \frac{b(s-b)}{c(s-c)} $  $ \therefore \prod_{cyc} \frac{A_0B}{A_0C} = -\prod_{cyc} \frac{b(s-b)}{c(s-c)} = -1 $ Hence by Menelaus theorem we get  $ A_0 , B_0 , C_0 $  are collinear.**<span style="color:#f00">Lemma 2 : </span>** Let  $ D,E,F $  be the intouch points of  $ \triangle ABC $ . And let  $ EF \cap BC = T_a , FD \cap CA = T_b $  and  $ DE \cap AB = T_c $ . Then  $IH$  radical axis of  $ \odot (AT_a) , \odot (BT_b) , \odot (CT_c) $ .
Proof :
Let  $ D' ,  E' , F' $  be midpoints of  $ EF, FD, DE $  respectively. And let  $ H_a , H_b , H_c $  be foot of perpendiculars from  $ A , B , C $  to  $ BC, CA, AB $  respectively.
Observe that  $ H_a , D' \in \odot (AT_a) $  similarly  $ H_b , E' \in \odot (BT_b) $  and  $ H_c , F' \in \odot (CT_c) $ .
Now we known that  $ HH_a \cdot HA = HH_b \cdot HB = HH_c \cdot HC $  
and  $ IA \cdot ID' = IB \cdot IE' = IC \cdot IF' $  
Thus we get  $ IH $  is radical axis of  $ \odot (AT_a) , \odot (BT_b) , \odot (CT_c) $ .

Denote by  $Y_a := $  circumcenter of  $ \odot (AT_a ) $ . Similarly define  $ Y_b , Y_c $ Now we have  $ Y_a , Y_b , Y_c $  collinear as  $ \odot (AT_a) , \odot (BT_b) , \odot (CT_c) $  are coaxial circles. Moreover as  $IH$  is their radical axis we get  $\boxed{IH \perp \overline{ \rm Y_aY_bY_c } }$ So to show that  $ \overline{ \rm A_0B_0C_0  } \perp IH $ , it is equivalent to  $ \overline{ \rm A_0B_0C_0 } \parallel \overline{ \rm Y_aY_bY_c} $ Let  $ T_a  = ( 0 : y : z )  $  , then as  $ E,F,T_a $  are collinear $\Rightarrow \begin{vmatrix} 0 & y & z  s-c & 0 & s-a  s-b & s-a & 0 \end{vmatrix} = 0 $  $ \Rightarrow y : z = -(s-c) : (s-b) $  $ \boxed{\therefore T_a = ( 0 : - (s-c) : s-b )} $ Then  $ Y_a := $  is midpoint of  $ AT_a = (c-b : -(s-c) : (s-b ))$ Similarly  $ Y_b = ( s-c : a-c : -(s-a) )$ , thus  $ \boxed{(Y_aY_b)_{\infty} = ( -(s-a)(c-b) : -(a-c)(s-b) : (a-b)(s-c) ) }$ It is enough to show that  $ A_0 , B_0 $  and  $ (Y_aY_b)_{\infty} $  are collinear. $ \Leftrightarrow \begin{vmatrix}  -(s-a)(c-b) & -(a-c)(s-b) & (a-b)(s-c)  0 & c(s-c) & -b(s-b)  c(s-c) & 0 & -a(s-a) \end{vmatrix} = 0 $ Now applying  $ C_3 \to C_1 + C_2 + C_3 $   $\Leftrightarrow  \begin{vmatrix}  -(s-a)(c-b) & -(a-c)(s-b) & 0  0 & c(s-c) & (b-c)(s-a)  c(s-c) & 0 & (a-c)(s-b) \end{vmatrix} = 0 $ Now simply expanding the determinant gives us  $ \begin{vmatrix}  -(s-a)(c-b) & -(a-c)(s-b) & 0  0 & c(s-c) & (b-c)(s-a)  c(s-c) & 0 & (a-c)(s-b) \end{vmatrix} = (s-a)(s-b)(s-c)(b-c)(a-c)c - (s-a)(s-b)(s-c)(b-c)(a-c)c =0 $ Hence  $ \overline{ \rm A_0B_0C_0 } \parallel \overline{ \rm Y_aY_bY_c } \Rightarrow \boxed{ \overline{ \rm A_0B_0C_0 } \perp IH } $ Hence, proved.

