We have  $a_4=5m-1, a_6=5m^3+8m^2-2m-4$ . Then  $4a_4a_6$  should be a perfect square, but for  $m > 10$  we have
\[(10m^2+7m-7)^2 < 4a_4a_6 < (10m^2+7m-6)^2.\]
We are left to check  $2\leq m\leq 10$ . Only  $m=2, 10$  make  $a_4$  a perfect square. We claim that both of these cases work. For  $m=2$ , we have  $a_n=b_n^2$  where
\begin{align*}
b_1 &=b_2=1, 
b_n &= b_{n-1}+b_{n-2}
\end{align*}
(the Fibonacci numbers) and for  $m=10$ , we have  $a_n=b_n^2$  where
\begin{align*}
b_1 &=-1, \quad b_2=1, 
b_n &=3b_{n-1}+b_{n-2}.
\end{align*}
An easy way to prove this is to verify that both sides satisfy linear recurrences with the same characteristic polynomial and then check that the first three terms agree. <details><summary>Details</summary>The characteristic polynomial of  $\{a_n\}$  is  $(x+1)(x^2-(m+1)x+1)$ , so  $a_n=c_1r_1^n+c_2r_2^n+c_3(-1)^n$  where  $r_1+r_2=m+1,\, r_1r_2=1$ . Meanwhile, the sequence  $\{b_n\}$  with characteristic polynomial  $x^2-tx-1$  takes the form  $b_n=c_4s_1^n+c_5s_2^n$  with  $s_1+s_2=t, \, s_1s_2=-1$ . Then  $b_n^2=c_4^2s_1^{2n}+c_5^2s_2^{2n}+2c_4c_5(-1)^n$ . It turns out that  $\{r_1, r_2\}=\{s_1^2, s_2^2\}$  in these cases (this is equivalent to  $m=t^2+1$ ) so if  $a_n$  and  $b_n^2$  agree for three consecutive terms, they always agree. After observing that  $(b_1, b_2, b_3)=(1, 1, 2)$  in the first case and  $(b_1, b_2, b_3)=(-1, 1, 2)$  in the second case, we are done.</details>

We claim the only solution is  $f(x) \equiv x + 1$ , which can be easily checked to work. Now, the idea is the following double count: \begin{align*}
    f^{t + x + y + 1} (z) = f^{f^{f^{f(t)}(x)}(y)} (z) = f^{f^{f(f^{f(t) - 1} (x))}(y)} (z) = f^{f(t) - 1}(x) + y + z + 1.
\end{align*} Now, consider what happens if we take  $f$  of both sides. Observe that on one hand \begin{align*}
    f(f^{t + x + y + 1} (z)) = f^{t + x + (y + 1) + 1} (z) = f^{f(t) - 1} (x) + y + z + 2.
\end{align*} On the other hand, we have \begin{align*}
    f(f^{f(t) - 1}(x) + y + z + 1) = f^{t + x + y + 2}(z) = f^{f(t) - 1}(x) + y + z + 2.
\end{align*} By varying  $y$ , we find that for sufficiently large values of  $y$ ,  $f(y) \equiv y + 1$ . 

Now, let  $a$  and  $b$  be sufficiently big so that  $f(a) = a + 1$  and  $f(b) = b + 1$ . Notice that by adding  $1$ , we remain in the ``sufficiently big" set, so  $f^k(a) = a + k$  for any  $k$ . Consider  $P(x, a, b):$  \begin{align*}
    x + a + b + 1 = f^{f^{f(x)}(a)} (b) = b + f^{f(x)} (a) = b + a + f(x),
\end{align*} implying  $f(x) \equiv x + 1$  for any  $x$ , as desired.

We claim that the only solution is  $\boxed{f(x)\equiv x+1}$ , which clearly works. Now, let  $P(x,y,z)$  be the assertion. Firstly, fix  $x$ ,  $y$ , and assume that there exists  $z_1\neq z_2$  such that  $f(z_1)=f(z_2)$ . We have that,
\begin{align*}
P(x,y,z_1),P(x,y,z_2)\implies f^{f^{f(x)}(y)}(z_1)=f^{f^{f(x)}(y)}(z_2)\implies z_1=z_2,
\end{align*}
which is a contradiction, implying that  $f$  is injective.Suppose that there exists  $n$  such that  $f(n)=1$ , then  $P(n,n,n)$  yields contradiction.
\begin{align*}
P(x,y,1), P(y,x,1)\implies f^{f^{f(x)}(y)}(1) = x+y+2=f^{f^{f(y)}(x)}(1)
\end{align*}
If  $f^{f(x)}(y)\neq f^{f(y)}(x)$ , then we can cancel the iteration due to injectivity and get that  $f(n)=1$  for some  $n$ , which is a contradiction. Therefore,  $f^{f(x)}(y)=f^{f(y)}(x)$ . Now, plugging  $y=1$  to this equation, we see that  $f^{f(x)}(1)=f^{f(1)}(x)$ . If  $f(x)<f(1)$  we can similarly cancel the iteration and get a contradiction, so  $f(1)$  is the minimum value of this function. Plugging  $x=1$  and  $y=2$  to this equation, we see that  $f^{f(2)-f(1)}(1)=2$ , implying that  $f(1)=2$ . Then,  $f^{f(2)-2}(1)=2=f^1(1)$ , so  $f(2)=3$ . 
\begin{align*}
P(1,1,x)\implies f^{f^{f(1)}(1)}(x)=x+3\implies f^3(x)=x+3\implies f(x+3)=f^4(x)=f(x)+3
\end{align*}
Since  $x=y=z=1$  implies that there exist  $n$  such that  $f(n)=4$ , it is easy to see that  $f(3)=4$  and that  $f(x)=x+1$  as desired  $\square$

The answer is yes. We are given that for every  $m\in \mathbb{N}$ , there exists a Fibonacci number that is  $k\pmod m$ , with  $k$  fixed. Consider the modulus  $m=F_{2N}+F_{2N-2}$  for some  $N$ . All equivalences that follow will be in mod  $m$ . **<span style="color:#00f">Claim:</span>**  $F_{2N+a} \equiv (-1)^{a+1}F_{2N-a-2}$  for all  $a\ge 0$ .

*<span style="color:#00f">Proof:</span>* This is easy to see by induction, base case  $a=0$  obvious. We have
\begin{align*}
     F_{2N+a+2}&=F_{2N+a+1}+F_{2N+a} 
     &\equiv (-1)^{a+2} F_{2N-a-3} + (-1)^{a+1} F_{2N-a-2} 
     &\equiv (-1)^a [F_{2N-a-3} - F_{2N-a-2}] 
     &\equiv (-1)^{a+3} F_{2N-a-4}.
\end{align*}
This completes the induction.  $\square$ Now, the residues of  $(F_0,\ldots,F_{4N-3})$  mod  $m$  are 
\[  (F_0, F_1,\ldots, F_{2N-1}, -F_{2N-2}, F_{2N-3}, -F_{2N-4}, F_{2N-5},\ldots, -F_2, F_1). \]
Therefore,  $F_{4N-2}\equiv F_0$ ,  $F_{4N-1} \equiv F_1$ . This means that the sequence  $(F_i \mod m)$  for  $i\ge 0$  is periodic with period  $4N-2$ . So actually all the residues possible are in the list above. 

Now, just take  $N$  large enough so that  $k+F_{2N-2} < m$ , i.e.  $k<F_{2N}$ . Then, we can never have  $k\equiv -F_{2N-2\ell}$  for any  $\ell=1,\ldots,N-1$  since  $k+F_{2N-2\ell} < m=F_{2N}+F_{2N-2}$ . This forces  $k\equiv F_\ell$  for some  $0\le \ell \le 2N-1$ . But since  $F_{\ell},k < F_{2N} < m$ , this actually forces  $k=F_{\ell}$ . The end.

