We use cartesian coordinates. Let  $D$  be the origin, and let  $A=(0,1),$   $B=(b,0),$  and  $C=(c,0).$  Note that  $\triangle ABC$  cannot be isosceles, nor can it be right, so this requires that  $b+c\neq 0$  and  $bc\neq 0,$  respectively.

First, we compute  $H.$  Since  $AC$  is parametrized by  $y=-\tfrac{1}{c}x+1,$  the altitude from  $B$  to  $AC$  is parametrized by  $y=cx-bc,$  meaning that  $H$  has coordinates  $(0,-bc).$  Next, we compute  $(AHP).$  The reflection of  $D$  over  $M=\left(\tfrac{b+c}{2},0\right)$  has coordinates  $(b+c,0),$  so the line  $HD'$  is parametrized by  $y=\tfrac{bc}{b+c}x-bc.$  Intersecting this with  $y=1,$  we find that  $P$  has coordinates  $\left(\tfrac{(1+bc)(b+c)}{bc},1\right).$  Thus,  $(AHP)$  is parametrized by
\begin{align*}
\left(x-\tfrac{(1+bc)(b+c)}{2bc}\right)^2+\left(y-\tfrac{1-bc}{2}\right)^2&=\left(\tfrac{(1+bc)(b+c)}{2bc}\right)^2+\left(\tfrac{1+bc}{2}\right)^2 
x^2-\tfrac{(1+bc)(b+c)}{bc}x+y^2-(1-bc)y&=bc.\qquad (\spadesuit)
\end{align*}By orthocenter reflections, to find  $(BHC)$ 's parametrization, it suffices to find the parametrization of  $(ABC)$  itself because the two circles are reflections over  $BC.$  We find that the circumcenter of  $\triangle ABC$ , also the midpoint of  $D'H,$  can be computed to be  $\left(\tfrac{b+c}{2},\tfrac{1+bc}{2}\right),$  so  $(BHC)$  is parametrized by
\begin{align*}
\left(x-\tfrac{b+c}{2}\right)^2+\left(y+\tfrac{1+bc}{2}\right)^2&=\tfrac{(b+c)^2(1+b^2)(1+c^2)}{(b+c+\sqrt{1+b^2}+\sqrt{1+c^2})(-b-c+\sqrt{1+b^2}+\sqrt{1+c^2})(b+c-\sqrt{1+b^2}+\sqrt{1+c^2})(b+c+\sqrt{1+b^2}-\sqrt{1+c^2})} 
\left(x-\tfrac{b+c}{2}\right)^2+\left(y+\tfrac{1+bc}{2}\right)^2&=\tfrac{(b+c)^2(1+b^2)(1+c^2)}{-((b+c+\sqrt{1+b^2})^2-(\sqrt{1+c^2})^2)((b+c-\sqrt{1+b^2})^2-(\sqrt{1+c^2})^2)} 
\left(x-\tfrac{b+c}{2}\right)^2+\left(y+\tfrac{1+bc}{2}\right)^2&=\tfrac{(b+c)^2(1+b^2)(1+c^2)}{-(2b^2+2bc+2(b+c)\sqrt{1+b^2})(2b^2+2bc-2(b+c)\sqrt{1+b^2})} 
\left(x-\tfrac{b+c}{2}\right)^2+\left(y+\tfrac{1+bc}{2}\right)^2&=\tfrac{(b+c)^2(1+b^2)(1+c^2)}{-4((b^2+bc)^2-((b+c)\sqrt{1+b^2})^2)} 
\left(x-\tfrac{b+c}{2}\right)^2+\left(y+\tfrac{1+bc}{2}\right)^2&=\tfrac{(b+c)^2(1+b^2)(1+c^2)}{-4(\cancel{b^4}+\cancel{2b^3c}+\cancel{b^2c^2}-b^2-2bc-c^2-\cancel{b^4}-\cancel{2b^3c}-\cancel{b^2c^2})} 
\left(x-\tfrac{b+c}{2}\right)^2+\left(y+\tfrac{1+bc}{2}\right)^2&=\tfrac{(b+c)^2(1+b^2)(1+c^2)}{4(b+c)^2} 
\left(x-\tfrac{b+c}{2}\right)^2+\left(y+\tfrac{1+bc}{2}\right)^2&=\tfrac{(1+b^2)(1+c^2)}{4} 
x^2-(b+c)x+y^2+(1+bc)y&=-bc,\qquad(\clubsuit)
\end{align*}where the right hand side comes from  $\tfrac{abc}{4R}=[ABC]=\sqrt{s(s-a)(s-b)(s-c)}.$ We now compute  $G,$  which is where things start to get ugly oops. Subtracting  $(\clubsuit)$  from  $(\spadesuit),$  we get
\begin{align*}
\tfrac{-(b+c)}{bc}x-2y&=2bc 
y&=-bc-\tfrac{b+c}{2bc}x,\qquad (\diamondsuit)
\end{align*}so plugging this into  $(\clubsuit)$  gives
\begin{align*}
x^2-(b+c)x+\left(-bc-\tfrac{b+c}{2bc}x\right)^2+(1+bc)\left(-bc-\tfrac{b+c}{2bc}x\right)&=-bc 
\left(1+\tfrac{(b+c)^2}{4b^2c^2}\right)x^2-\tfrac{(1+bc)(b+c)}{2bc}x+\cancel{bc}+\cancel{b^2c^2}-\cancel{bc}-\cancel{b^2c^2}&=0 
x((4b^2c^2+(b+c)^2)x-2bc(b+c+b^2c+bc^2))&=0, 
\end{align*}which implies that  $x=0,\tfrac{2bc(1+bc)(b+c)}{4b^2c^2+(b+c)^2}.$  Notice that the denominator for the latter solution cannot equal  $0$  because  $4b^2c^2$  and  $(b+c)^2$  are both positive, so both solutions are valid; the former solution corresponds to  $H,$  which means that  $G$  has coordinates
\[\left(\tfrac{2bc(1+bc)(b+c)}{4b^2c^2+(b+c)^2},-\tfrac{4b^3c^3+2bc(b+c)^2+(b+c)^2}{4b^2c^2+(b+c)^2}\right)\]upon plugging our  $x$  value into  $(\diamondsuit).$  Finally, the slope of  $GH$  is
\[
\tfrac{-\tfrac{4b^3c^3+2bc(b+c)^2+(b+c)^2}{4b^2c^2+(b+c)^2}+bc}{\tfrac{2bc(1+bc)(b+c)}{4b^2c^2+(b+c)^2}-0}=-\tfrac{(1+bc)(b+c)^2}{2bc(1+bc)(b+c)} =-\tfrac{b+c}{2bc},
\]which is evidently the negative reciprocal of  $MH$ 's slope  $\tfrac{0+bc}{\tfrac{b+c}{2}-0}=\tfrac{2bc}{b+c}$  as desired.  $\blacksquare$ -----
