<blockquote>Given two circles  $\omega_1,\omega_2$  tangent at  $H$ . Consider any point  $M$  and let  $G^*$  be the intersection of poles of  $M$  wrt  $\omega_1,\omega_2$ . Show that  $H$  lies on  $\omega$ , the circle with diameter  $\overline{MG^*}$ .</blockquote>
Let  $\Psi$  denote inversion wrt  $\omega$ . Note inversion wrt both of  $\omega_1,\omega_2$  fixes  $\omega$ . In particular  $\Psi$  fixes both  $\omega_1,\omega_2$ . So the center of  $\omega$  (say  $O$ ), lies on the radical axes of  $\omega_1,\omega_2$ , which is their common tangent at  $H$ . Again as  $\Psi$  fixes  $\omega_1,\omega_2$ , so radius of  $\omega$  must be  $|OH|$ , implying  $H \in \omega$ .  $\blacksquare$

