Now we prove the bound. If there is not a  $1$  in the grid, decrease every number by the same amount until there is. Now that there is a  $1$  in the grid, the largest number in its column is  $m$  – therefore, the smallest number in each row is at most  $m$ . Similarly, the smallest number in each column is at most  $n$ .

Let  $a_k$  denote the number of rows whose smallest element is  $k$ , for  $1 \leq k \leq m$ . Let  $b_k$  denote the number of columns whose smallest element is  $k$ , for  $1 \leq k \leq n$ . Let  $g_k$  denote the number of instances of  $k$  in the grid. We have the following equations:
\begin{align*} b_1 &= g_1 = a_1 
b_2 + b_1 &= g_2 = a_2 + a_1 
\vdots ~ ~ ~ & ~ ~ ~ \vdots \vdots \vdots \qquad ~ ~ \vdots 
b_m + \dots + b_1 &= g_m = a_m + \dots + a_1 
b_{m+1} + \dots + b_2 &= g_{m+1} = a_m + \dots + a_1 
\vdots ~ ~ ~ & ~ ~ ~ \vdots \vdots \vdots \qquad ~ ~ \vdots 
b_n + \dots + b_{n-m+1} &= g_n = a_m + \dots + a_1 
b_n + \dots + b_{n-m+2} &= g_{n+1} = a_m + \dots + a_2 
\vdots ~ ~ ~ & ~ ~ ~ \vdots \vdots \vdots \qquad ~ ~ \vdots 
b_n &= g_{m+n-1} = a_m.
\end{align*}
Subtracting adjacent pairs of equations gives us  $b_1 = a_1$ ,  $b_2 = a_2$ , and so on. In particular, define  $c_k = a_k$  for  $1 \leq k \leq m$  and  $c_k = b_{k-m}$  for  $m+1 \leq k \leq m+n$  – take all other indices modulo  $m+n$ . Then, by subtracting adjacent pairs of equations above, we precisely get
\[c_k = c_{k+m}\]
for all integers  $k$ . This means that  $c_1, c_2, \dots, c_{\gcd(m,n)}$  determine the whole sequence, and in particular, 
\[a_{m-\gcd(m,n)+1} = c_{m - \gcd(m,n) + 1} = c_1 = a_1 \geq 1,\]
so some row has a smallest element of  $m-\gcd(m,n)+1$ . This same row contains  $m+n - \gcd(m,n)$ , as desired.

We claim the answer is  $m+n-\gcd(m,n)$ . To achieve  $s=k$  for  $k\times k$ , take the entry in the  $i$ th row and  $j$ th entry to be  $i+j-1$ . Now to achieve  $s=m+n-\gcd(m,n)$  for  $m\times n$ , divide the grid into a  $\frac{m}{\gcd(m,n)}\times\frac{n}{\gcd(m,n)}$  metagrid of  $\gcd(m,n)\times \gcd(m,n)$  subgrids. Take the entries in the  $i$ th row and  $j$ th column of the metagrid to be the construction for a  $\gcd(m,n)\times\gcd(m,n)$  grid with  $\gcd(m,n)(i+j-2)$  added to each entry.

We now show that the maximum value in any grid must be at least  $m+n-\gcd(m,n)$ .  We have that the  $n$  entries in each row are distinct modulo  $n$ , and repeating over every row yields that there are  $m$  instances of every residue modulo  $n$ . Now let  $t_i+1$  be the minimum entry in the  $i$ th column; then the entries in that column are \[t_i+1,t_i+2,\dots,t_i+m\] Now consider the generating function for the grid formed by taking the sum of the monomials  $x^v$  for the value  $v$  in each cell; taking the exponents modulo  $n$ , we find that \[(x^{t_1}+x^{t_2}+\dots+x^{t_n})(x+x^2+\dots+x^m)\equiv m(1+x+\dots+x^{n-1})\pmod{x^n-1}\] both represent this generating function. We find
\begin{align*}
&(x+x^2+\dots+x^m)(x^{t_1}+x^{t_2}+\dots+x^{t_n})\equiv m(1+x+\dots+x^{n-1})\pmod{x^n-1}
\implies& (1+x+\dots+x^{n-1})\mid (x+x^2+\dots+x^m)(x^{t_1}+x^{t_2}+\dots+x^{t_n})
\implies& x^n-1\mid (x^m-1)(x^{t_1}+x^{t_2}+\dots+x^{t_n})
\implies& \frac{x^n-1}{\gcd(x^n-1,x^m-1)}\mid x^{t_1}+x^{t_2}+\dots+x^{t_n}
\implies& \frac{x^n-1}{x^{\gcd(m,n)}-1}\mid x^{t_1}+x^{t_2}+\dots+x^{t_n}
\end{align*}
Then since  $\frac{x^n-1}{x^{\gcd(m,n)}-1}$  has degree  $n-\gcd(m,n)$ , at least one of the  $t_i$  must be at least  $n-\gcd(m,n)$ . But then the largest entry in that column is at least  $m+n-\gcd(m,n)$ , as desired.

