credit to ok1985. $\color{blue} \textbf{\text{Answer.}}$   $\boxed{n=4k}$ . $\color{blue} \textbf{\text{Bound.}}$  We claim that we must have that  $n\geqslant 4k$ .
For an even  $n$ , just divide the grid into  $2\times 2$  squares and note that in every such square has at most  $1$  black square, therefore we must have  $n\geqslant 4k$ .
Thus, let us fix  $k$  and consider still that same  $n$ . Now, biggest  $\text{odd}\times\text{odd}$  grid, which is smaller than  $n$  is  $2m-1$ , where  $n=2m$ .

So let us consider  $(2m-1)\times (2m-1)$  grid and we must have in each row and column,  $k$  black squares.
