Write  $\mho(n,k)$  in summation form. Then, we have 
\begin{align*}
\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \sum_{k=1}^{\infty} \sum_{p \ge k,\phantom{2} p \mid n} \frac{1}{3^{n+k-7}} &= \sum_p \sum_{p \mid n} \sum_{p \ge k\ge1} \frac{1}{3^{n+k-7}}
\end{align*}
since we can think of this as: for a prime  $p$ , if  $p\mid n$  and  $k$  is less than  $p$ , we can sum the value  $\tfrac{1}{3^{n+k-7}}.$  Thus, 
\begin{align*}
\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \sum_{k=1}^{\infty} \sum_{p \ge k,\phantom{2} p \mid n} \frac{1}{3^{n+k-7}} &= \sum_p \sum_{p \mid n} \sum_{p \ge k\ge1} \frac{1}{3^{n+k-7}}
&= 3^7\sum_p \frac{1}{3^n}\sum_{p\mid n} \frac{3^p-1}{2\cdot 3^p}
&= 3^7 \sum_p \frac{3^p -1}{2 \cdot 3^p} \sum_{a\ge 1} \frac{1}{3^{pa}}
&= 3^7\sum_p \frac{3^p-1}{2\cdot 3^p} \cdot \frac{\tfrac{1}{3^p}}{1-\tfrac{1}{3^p}}
&=0.5\sum_p3^{7-p}.
\end{align*}
Where we simplify by geometric series twice. Then, we have the sum as  $$ \frac{1}{2}\left(3^5+3^4+3^2+3^0 + 3^{-4} + 3^{-6} +\cdots\right) = \frac{1}{2}(334 + 3^{-4} +3^{-6}) < 167.5 $$  so the answer is  $\boxed{167}.$

Standard summation tricks never get old!
\begin{align*}
\sum_{n, k \geq 1} \frac{\mho(n, k)}{3^{n+k-7}} &= \sum_{n, k \geq 1} \frac{\sum_{p \mid n, p \geq k} 1}{3^{n+k-1}}
& = \sum_{p \ \mathrm{ prime}} \underbrace{\frac 1{3^{p+1-7}} + \frac 1{3^{p+2-7}} + \cdots + \frac 1{3^{p+p-7}}}_{\mho(p, k \leq p)} + \underbrace{\frac 1{3^{2p+1-7}} + \frac 1{3^{2p+2-7}} + \cdots + \frac 1{3^{2p+p-7}}}_{\mho(2p, k \leq p)} + \cdots 
&= \sum_{p \ \mathrm{prime}} \frac{\frac 1{3^{p-6}}}{1-\frac 13} 
&= \frac 32 \sum_{p \ \mathrm{ prime}} \frac 1{3^{p-6}} 
&\approx \frac 32\left(\frac 1{3^{2-6}} + \frac 1{3^{3-6}} + \frac 1{3^{5-6}} + \frac 1{3^{7-6}}\right) 
&= \boxed{167}.
\end{align*}

Really nice problem.
Let  $\mathbb{P}$  be the set of primes. We calculate that
\begin{align*}
\sum_{n = 1}^\infty \sum_{k=1}^\infty \frac{\mho(n,k)}{3^{n+k-7}}
&=
3^7\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\sum_{k=1}^{\infty}
\sum_{\substack{k\leq i i \vert n, i \in \mathbb{P}}}\frac{1}{3^{n+k}}
=
3^7\sum_{i \in \mathbb{P}}\sum_{i\vert n}\sum_{k=1}^{i}\frac{1}{3^{n+k}}
&=
3^7\sum_{i \in \mathbb{P}}\sum_{i\vert n}\frac{1}{3^n}\sum_{k=1}^i\frac{1}{3^k}
=
3^7\sum_{i \in \mathbb{P}}\sum_{i\vert n}\frac{1}{3^n}\left(\frac12\left(1-\frac{1}{3^i}\right)\right)
&= \frac{3^7}{2}\sum_{i \in \mathbb{P}}\sum_{j=1}^{\infty}\frac{1}{3^{ij}}-\frac{1}{3^{i(j+1)}} = \frac{3^7}{2}\sum_{i \in \mathbb{P}}\frac{1}{3^i}
\end{align*}
The terms when  $i \geq 11$  are irrelevant, so we calculate
\[
\frac{3^7}{2}\left(\frac{1}{3^2}+\frac{1}{3^3}+\frac{1}{3^5}+\frac{1}{3^7}\right) = \boxed{167}
\]

We have
\begin{align*}
& \sum_{n=1}^\infty \sum_{k=1}^\infty \frac{\mho(n,k)}{3^{n+k-7}} 
=& \sum_{k=1}^\infty\sum_{p=k}^\infty\sum_{p\mid n} \frac{1}{3^{n+k-7}} 
=& \sum_{k=1}^\infty\frac{1}{3^{k-7}}\sum_{p\ge k,p\text{ prime}}\frac{1}{3^p-1} 
=& 3^{7}\sum_{p\text{ prime}}\frac{1}{3^p-1}\sum_{k=1}^{p}\frac{1}{3^k} 
=& 3^{7}\sum_{p\text{ prime}}\frac{1}{3^p-1}\cdot\frac{1/3-1/3^{p+1}}{1-1/3} 
=& 3^{7}\sum_{p\text{ prime}}\frac{1}{3^p-1}\cdot\frac{1-3^{-p}}{2} 
=& \frac{3^7}{2}\sum_{p\text{ prime}}\frac{1}{3^p}. 
\end{align*}
Note that the sum in base  $3$  looks like  $0.01101010001\dots$ , so multiplying by  $3^7$  gives  $110101.0001\dots$  which is nearest to the even integer  $334$ , so the answer is  $\boxed{167}$ .

Let  $p$  be a prime. We will compute the sum of all values for which  $p$  divides  $n$ . Then, we want to compute the sum of  $p$  over all primes:
\begin{align*}
3^7 \cdot \sum_{\alpha = 1}^{\infty} \dfrac{1}{3^{p\alpha}} \cdot \sum_{k = 1}^{p} \dfrac{1}{3^k} = 3^7 \cdot \dfrac{\tfrac{1}{3^p}}{1 - \tfrac{1}{3^p}} \cdot \dfrac{\tfrac{1}{3}\cdot\left(1-\tfrac{1}{3^p}\right)}{1 - \tfrac{1}{3}} = \dfrac{3^{7-p}}{2}.
\end{align*}Therefore, the value we want is roughly the following:
