I found this to be pretty simple for #7: the Wilson pairing trick is pretty well-known.

Notice that  $2n-3 = -1$ , in which case  $n = 1$ , or  $2n-3$  must be prime. Write 
\begin{align*}
15n^2 &\equiv 15(1 \cdot 2 \cdots n)((n-3) \cdot (n-2) \cdots (2n-4)) \pmod {2n-3} 
&\equiv 15(-1)^n (2n-4)!(n-3)(n-2)(n-1)n \pmod {2n-3} 
&\equiv 15(-1)^{n+1}(n-3)(n-2)(n-1)n \pmod {2n-3}.
\end{align*}
Now we can just do polynomial division: notice that we can drop all the factors of  $2$  because  $2n-3$  is odd. If  $n$  is even, then   $$ 2n-3 \mid 15(n-3)(n-2)(n-1)n - 1 \implies 2n-3 \mid 119, $$   so  $n = 2$  and  $n= 10$  work here. If  $n$  is odd, then   $$ 2n-3 \mid 15(n-3)(n-2)(n-1)n + 1 \implies 2n-3 \mid 151, $$   so  $n = 77$  works here. Thus, the desired sum is  $77+2+10+1=\boxed{90}$ .

