Note that it suffices to show  $PT=PE$  and analogously  $PS=PF \;$ .We'll show only the first part now.By similar triangles we get  $PE=\dfrac{PA.PC}{AC}\; ,PT=\dfrac{PB.PD}{BD}$ .So we have to show  $\mathbb{P}=\left(\dfrac{PA\cdot PC}{PB\cdot PD}\cdot\dfrac{BD}{AC}\right)=1$ . 

Invert at  $P$  with arbitary radius.We get the following equivalent problem:-
<blockquote>Let  $P$  be a point.Let  $\ell$  be a line through  $P$ .Let  $\chi \parallel \ell$  be another line.Let  $A^*,B^*\in \chi$ .Let a circle  $\Gamma$  through  $A^*,B^*$  intersect  $\ell$  at  $D^*,C^*$ .Then  $P=1$ </blockquote>
