solution_md:
Invert around  $A$  with radius  $1.$  Let  $X^*$  denote the inverse of  $X.$  

Then, repeatedly using the facts that   $$ AX^*=\frac{1}{AX}, X^*Y^*=\frac{XY}{AX^* AY^*}, $$   we find that the desired inequality is equivalent to   $$ \frac{B^*D^* \cdot C^*E^* \cdot D^*F^* \cdot F^*B^*}{B^*C^* \cdot C^*D^* \cdot D^*E^* \cdot E^*F^*} \ge 27, $$   where  $F^*, E^*, D^*, C^*,$  and  $B^*$  are distinct points lying on a line in that order and  $A$  is some point not on that line. Letting  $x_1, x_2, x_3,$  and  $x_4$  denote the lengths of  $\overline{F^*E^*}, \overline{E^*D^*}, \overline{D^*C^*},$  and  $\overline{C^*B^*},$  respectively, this is equivalent to the inequality   $$ (x_1+x_2)(x_2+x_3)(x_3+x_4)(x_1+x_2+x_3+x_4) \ge 27x_1x_2x_3x_4 $$   over positive real numbers  $x_1, \ldots, x_4.$  Using the Weighted AM-GM Inequality, we have
\begin{align*}
    (x_1+x_2)(x_2+x_3)(x_3+x_4)(x_1+x_2+x_3+x_4) &\ge 3\sqrt[3]{\frac{x_1^2x_2}{4}} \cdot 2\sqrt{x_2x_3} \cdot 3\sqrt[3]{\frac{x_3x_4^2}{4}} \cdot 6\sqrt[6]{\frac{x_1^2x_2x_3x_4^2}{16}} 
    &= 27x_1x_2x_3x_4,
\end{align*}
concluding the proof.  $\square$

